<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Jamrud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghan-sikh-wars">Afghan–Sikh Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Jamrud was fought on 30 April 1837 at the fort of Jamrud, at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> and an Afghan army sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa">Hari Singh Nalwa</a> was killed, but the garrison held out until relief came, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> withdrew. The battle confirmed Sikh control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> and marked the limit of Sikh expansion to the west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-jamrud.jpg" alt="Battle of Jamrud" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Annie Brassey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamrud_Fort_-_pg_18_-The_last_voyage_-_Annie_Brassey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After consolidating his empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> had taken advantage of the Afghan civil war to conquer long-held Afghan lands, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> lost the Punjab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, Derajat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a>, Balakot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a>. Peshawar was seized from the Peshawar Sardars in 1834. Towards the end of 1836 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa">Hari Singh Nalwa</a>, commander-in-chief of the Khalsa Army, captured the small fortified village of Jamrud at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> frontier up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1837 the best <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> generals and troops, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, were recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> for the wedding of Kanwar Nau Nihal Singh, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>'s grandson. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> seized the moment and sent his sons with 7,000 cavalry, 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> men, 9,000 irregulars and 20,000 Khyber tribesmen. Finding no Sikh army at Jamrud, Wazir Akbar Khan began to demolish the fort's defences, until Nalwa charged and threw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> into disarray. Shams al-Din Khan countercharged, and Akbar Khan rallied his men and drove the Sikhs back into the fort.</p><p>Accounts of Nalwa's death differ. The Afghan chronicle Siraj al-Tawarikh says he and Akbar Khan fought a duel without recognising each other, while the historian Hari Ram Gupta writes that he was struck by two bullets and carried into the fort, where he ordered his death kept secret until help came. The garrison held out until reinforcements arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> withdrew to Jalalabad. Historians disagree over the result: some count it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> victory, others stress Nalwa's death, and James Norris calls it inconclusive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Dost Mohammad wrote to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> that his sons had acted without authority, sent horses as gifts and proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> be entrusted to him, warning that otherwise he would resort to arms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> court refused. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> reorganised the government of Peshawar: General Paolo Avitabile became its governor, the surrounding districts went to the Barakzai Sardars and local chiefs, and a strong garrison was posted at the new fort of Shabkadar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Jamrud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That frontier was defended in 1837 at the <b>Battle of Jamrud</b>, where the general Hari Singh Nalwa was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
